<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Direct distribution (Developer ID, hardened runtime, notarised).

	     Deliberately empty. Everything the app links is static - SDL, ImGui,
	     libchdr - and the emulator is a plain interpreter with no generated
	     code, so it needs none of the usual hardened-runtime exceptions. Adding
	     them "just in case" is how an app ends up asking for JIT memory it never
	     uses, which is a question at review time and a weakening either way.

	     If notarisation or launch ever fails on a hardened-runtime complaint,
	     add the ONE entitlement the log names and record why here:
	       com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
	       com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
	       com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory

	     The App Sandbox is NOT enabled, and that is a decision rather than an
	     omission: the app's file browser walks the user's own disks to find a
	     BIOS and a disc library, and under the sandbox every one of those paths
	     reads as an empty folder. The Mac App Store requires the sandbox, so it
	     also requires an NSOpenPanel-plus-security-scoped-bookmark flow first -
	     the same shape as the Android SAF work. See docs/APPLE_BUILD.md. -->
</dict>
</plist>
